# Europe batch 2 (1609-1762): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"d-kepler": dict(
 bluf="Imperial mathematician Johannes Kepler set out to fit Tycho Brahe's measurements of Mars to the circles every astronomer assumed. The circles failed by eight minutes of arc, and he replaced them with ellipses. The first laws of planetary motion that matched observation made astronomy a physics and gave Newton the orbits his gravity had to explain.",
 now="Kepler replaced the circles of ancient astronomy with laws derived from measurement, which made Newton's physics possible sixty years later and set the method modern science uses: fit the theory to the data.",
 story="Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe died in 1601 and left his Mars observations to his assistant, German mathematician Johannes Kepler, who succeeded him as imperial mathematician in Prague. Kepler spent six years on the orbit. In 'Astronomia nova' of 1609 he published his first two laws: planets move in ellipses with the Sun at one focus, and sweep equal areas in equal times.",
 why="Copernicus's circles missed Tycho's Mars positions by eight minutes of arc, and Tycho's data were good to two. Kepler trusted the data over the circles.",
 then="The third law, relating orbital period to distance, followed in 1619. English mathematician Isaac Newton derived all three from gravity in 1687.",
 fact="Kepler's laws are still used to compute the orbits of planets, moons and spacecraft."),
"eu161030": dict(
 bluf="Paduan mathematician Galileo Galilei built a telescope to see what the Dutch spyglass could show of the sky, and to make his name. He found Jupiter's moons, the phases of Venus and mountains on the Moon, the first physical evidence for the Sun-centred model. His method of experiment and mathematics became the method of physics, and his clash with the Church became the defining story of evidence against authority.",
 now="Galileo's findings and his trial fixed the pattern of every later clash between evidence and authority. The Catholic Church acknowledged its error in 1992.",
 story="News of a Dutch spyglass reached Padua in 1609. Paduan mathematician Galileo Galilei built better ones and turned them on the sky: Jupiter's four moons, the phases of Venus, sunspots and mountains on the Moon. 'The Starry Messenger' of 1610 reported the findings in a style any educated reader could follow.",
 why="Europe was already arguing over Copernicus. Galileo believed in testing ideas by experiment and saw at once what the instrument could decide.",
 then="The Church banned Copernican books in 1616. Galileo's 'Dialogue' of 1632 appeared to mock Pope Urban VIII; he was tried in 1633 and confined to house arrest. His last book, on motion, was printed in Leiden in 1638.",
 fact="Galileo named Jupiter's moons the Medicean Stars to win patronage from the Grand Duke of Tuscany; it worked."),
"eu161831": dict(
 bluf="Habsburg Emperor Ferdinand II set out to restore Catholic and imperial authority over the Protestant princes; the princes and then Denmark, Sweden and France fought to stop him. Thirty years of war on German soil killed a third of the population in places and ended in a settlement that made the sovereign state, rather than religious unity, the basis of the European order. Germany stayed fragmented for two centuries.",
 now="The war fixed Germany's fragmentation until 1871 and made France the leading power in Europe. It is the model for every war in which outside powers fight their own contest on another country's ground.",
 story="Protestant nobles in Bohemia threw two imperial officials from a window of Prague Castle in May 1618 in protest at Habsburg curbs on their rights. Habsburg Emperor Ferdinand II crushed the revolt in 1620. The war drew in the Catholic League, the Protestant princes, Denmark, Sweden and in 1635 Catholic France, which fought on the Protestant side against the Habsburgs. Armies living off the land spread famine and plague across Germany.",
 why="The Peace of Augsburg of 1555 had left Calvinists out and church lands in dispute. The Habsburgs wanted a Catholic, centralised empire; the princes, Sweden and France wanted it weak.",
 trig="The Defenestration of Prague in May 1618.",
 then="Parts of Germany lost a third or more of their people. The Peace of Westphalia in 1648 gave the princes the right to conduct their own foreign policy, made France and Sweden great powers and removed religion as a cause of war between European states.",
 fact="The two officials thrown from the window survived the fall; Catholics credited angels, Protestants a dung heap."),
"d-whitemountain": dict(
 bluf="Habsburg Emperor Ferdinand II sent an army to recover Bohemia from the Protestant nobles who had elected a rival king. His troops routed them outside Prague in an hour. Bohemia lost its elective crown and its Protestant nobility, and the Habsburgs ruled it as a hereditary Catholic possession until 1918.",
 now="The defeat ended Bohemia's Protestant nobility and its elective crown; the Habsburgs re-Catholicised the kingdom and ruled it as a hereditary possession until 1918, which is why Czech nationalism defined itself against Vienna.",
 story="Bohemia's Protestant estates had rejected Habsburg Emperor Ferdinand II as king and elected Frederick V of the Palatinate in 1619. On 8 November 1620 imperial and Catholic League troops under Count Tilly routed Frederick's army on the White Mountain outside Prague. Frederick fled after one winter on the throne.",
 why="The estates had bet on Protestant Europe coming to their aid. Only a few thousand mercenaries came.",
 then="Twenty-seven rebel leaders were executed in Prague's Old Town Square in 1621. Their estates went to Catholic loyalists, many German-speaking, and Protestant worship was suppressed. Protestant princes across Germany armed for what came next.",
 fact="Frederick V reigned one winter and is remembered as the Winter King."),
"d-magdeburg": dict(
 bluf="Imperial commander Count Tilly besieged Magdeburg to punish a Lutheran city that had allied with Sweden. His troops broke in, and fire and plunder killed about 20,000 of its 25,000 people. The atrocity drove Saxony and Brandenburg into alliance with Sweden and turned the war against the emperor.",
 now="Magdeburg made the Protestant cause a coalition. The emperor's armies lost the field within four months and the chance to win the war outright.",
 story="Magdeburg, a leading Lutheran city, had allied with Swedish King Gustavus Adolphus and waited for relief that came too late. Imperial troops under Count Tilly and Gottfried von Pappenheim stormed the walls on 20 May 1631. Fire swept the city while the soldiers plundered and killed. About 20,000 died.",
 why="The city had defied the emperor and bet on Swedish rescue. Tilly's unpaid troops expected a sack.",
 then="Saxony and Brandenburg joined Sweden. Gustavus Adolphus routed Tilly at Breitenfeld in September 1631.",
 fact="The German verb 'magdeburgisieren' came to mean to destroy a place completely."),
"d-lutzen": dict(
 bluf="Swedish King Gustavus Adolphus attacked the imperial army under Albrecht von Wallenstein in fog near Leipzig to keep Saxony in the war. Sweden held the field and lost its king. Without him the Protestant cause lost its direction, the war dragged on for sixteen years, and France entered directly to hold the Habsburgs down.",
 now="Gustavus Adolphus's army of mobile guns, combined arms and drilled professionals became the model for every European army. Sweden stayed a great power in the Baltic until Poltava in 1709.",
 story="On 16 November 1632 Swedish King Gustavus Adolphus attacked imperial general Albrecht von Wallenstein's army in thick fog at Lützen in Saxony. The king rode into the mist at the head of his cavalry and was shot dead. His army took the field.",
 why="Sweden had entered the war in 1630 to save the German Protestants and to control the Baltic. Wallenstein's advance into Saxony threatened Sweden's main ally.",
 then="Chancellor Axel Oxenstierna kept Sweden in the war. France declared war on Spain in 1635 and took over the campaign in Germany.",
 fact="Wallenstein was assassinated on the emperor's orders fifteen months later, suspected of negotiating his own peace."),
"d-galileotrial": dict(
 bluf="Pope Urban VIII put Galileo Galilei on trial for defending the Copernican system after the 'Dialogue' of 1632 appeared to mock the Pope's own argument. Galileo abjured on his knees and spent his last nine years under house arrest. The Church had used its authority against a scientific claim, and scientific publishing moved north to Protestant Europe.",
 now="The trial moved the centre of science from Italy to the Netherlands and England. It remains the standard case of religious authority suppressing evidence, which the Church conceded in 1992.",
 story="Paduan mathematician Galileo Galilei published the 'Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems' in 1632 with the Pope's own argument in the mouth of a character named Simplicio. Pope Urban VIII, an old friend, took it as mockery. The Roman Inquisition found Galileo vehemently suspected of heresy in June 1633. He abjured and was confined to his villa near Florence.",
 why="A decree of 1616 had banned the Copernican doctrine. The Pope felt betrayed.",
 then="Galileo's last book, on motion, was smuggled out and printed in Leiden in 1638. French philosopher René Descartes shelved his own book on the Copernican universe.",
 myth="Galileo muttered 'and yet it moves' as he rose from his knees. The story first appears more than a century after the trial."),
"d-descartes": dict(
 bluf="French philosopher René Descartes set out to rebuild knowledge on reason alone, after religious war and the Galileo trial had discredited inherited authority. He found one certainty, his own thinking, and built a method of systematic doubt and a new geometry on it. Modern philosophy and the mathematics of science both descend from the book, and his choice to publish in the Dutch Republic shows where thought was free.",
 now="Descartes's method gave the new science a philosophy and the Dutch Republic the role of Europe's press. The mind-body problem he posed still frames philosophy and neuroscience.",
 story="French philosopher René Descartes, living in the Dutch Republic, published the 'Discourse on the Method' at Leiden in 1637, in French so that ordinary readers could follow. Doubting everything he could, he found one certainty: 'I think, therefore I am.' He had shelved an earlier book on the Copernican universe after hearing of Galileo's condemnation.",
 why="The new science needed a method. Decades of religious war had made certainty precious and Dutch presses safe.",
 then="Cartesian philosophy and analytic geometry shaped science and mathematics for a century.",
 fact="Every x-y graph uses Cartesian coordinates, named after him."),
"eu164232": dict(
 bluf="English King Charles I tried to govern and tax without Parliament and to impose his church on three kingdoms; Parliament raised an army to stop him. Parliament's New Model Army won, and the king was executed. The wars settled, after one more crisis in 1688, that English monarchs rule under law and with Parliament's consent.",
 now="Parliamentary sovereignty and the rule that representatives must approve taxes descend from the wars. The American revolutionaries read their own cause in it.",
 story="English King Charles I ruled without Parliament from 1629 to 1640, raising money by contested means and pushing church reforms that Puritans read as Catholic. His attempt to impose a prayer book on Scotland started a war he could pay for only by recalling Parliament. In 1642 both sides raised armies. Parliament's New Model Army under Oliver Cromwell won by 1646 and again in 1648.",
 why="Taxation without consent came first. Religion, fear of Catholicism against Puritan radicalism, gave each side its believers. The king's claim to rule by divine right left no room for compromise.",
 trig="Charles entered the Commons with soldiers to arrest five members in January 1642 and found them gone.",
 then="Charles I was executed in 1649. England became a republic under Cromwell, who conquered Ireland. The monarchy returned in 1660; Parliament's supremacy was fixed in 1688.",
 fact="About 4 per cent of England's population died in the wars, a higher share than in the First World War."),
"d-munster": dict(
 bluf="Spain wanted to end an eighty-year war and turn its forces on France; the Dutch wanted recognition. At Münster in 1648 Spanish King Philip IV recognised the seven United Provinces as sovereign and let them keep their conquests in Asia and the Americas. A trading republic became a sovereign power, and Spain accepted the loss of northern Europe.",
 now="The Dutch Republic's recognition, part of the Peace of Westphalia, helped establish the idea that sovereign states, not dynasties or the Church, are the basic units of international order.",
 story="The Dutch and Spain had fought since 1568. The Peace of Münster, signed on 30 January 1648 during the Westphalia negotiations, ended the war. Spain recognised the republic's independence and its colonial conquests and closed the Scheldt to Antwerp's trade, which left Amsterdam the port of northern Europe.",
 why="Spain was exhausted and at war with France. The republic had won its war at sea and in the colonies.",
 then="The Dutch golden age ran for another quarter century as Europe's leading trading nation.",
 fact="The closing of the Scheldt kept Antwerp a dead port until 1863."),
"eu164833": dict(
 bluf="Every power in the Thirty Years' War wanted out, and none could win. Five years of negotiation in two towns produced treaties that let each German prince run his own foreign policy, extended toleration to Calvinists, recognised the Dutch and Swiss, and paid France and Sweden in territory. The sovereign state replaced religious unity as the basis of European order, and the Holy Roman Empire became a shell.",
 now="Every argument over sovereignty and intervention since, from Kosovo to Syria, is conducted in Westphalian terms. China and Russia cite the principle of non-interference in the treaties' name.",
 story="Negotiators met at Münster and Osnabrück from 1644; Catholics and Protestants sat in separate towns. The treaties signed in October 1648 confirmed the princes' right to conduct their own foreign policy, extended the settlement of 1555 to Calvinists, recognised Dutch and Swiss independence and gave Alsace to France and Pomerania to Sweden.",
 why="Thirty years of war had exhausted every treasury. France and Sweden had won enough to dictate terms and too little to rule Germany.",
 then="The Holy Roman Empire became a loose association of some 300 territories. France became the leading power in Europe. Religion ceased to be a cause of war between European states.",
 myth="Westphalia invented the sovereign state. The treaties confirmed powers the princes already used; the doctrine was read back into them in the 1800s.",
 fact="The negotiations ran for five years and the question of who should enter a room first took months."),
"eu164934": dict(
 bluf="The army's leaders decided that a king who had restarted the war in 1648 could never be trusted with a settlement. A purged Parliament tried English King Charles I for treason against his people and beheaded him. A reigning king had been killed by law in the people's name, and the precedent ran to Louis XVI in 1793.",
 now="The execution set the precedent that a ruler can be tried by his subjects. The French Revolution followed it, and it remains the clearest case of a monarch held to account by law.",
 story="After losing two civil wars, English King Charles I was tried by a High Court of Justice set up by the Rump Parliament, the remnant left after the army's purge. He refused to recognise the court. He was beheaded outside the Banqueting House in Whitehall on 30 January 1649.",
 why="Charles had negotiated with Parliament while plotting with the Scots, and the army decided no settlement with him would hold.",
 then="England, Scotland and Ireland became a republic, and Oliver Cromwell ruled as Lord Protector from 1653. The monarchy returned in 1660 and the surviving regicides were executed.",
 fact="Charles wore two shirts to the scaffold so that the January cold would not make him shiver and seem afraid."),
"eu165135": dict(
 bluf="English philosopher Thomas Hobbes wrote during the civil war to show why any government is better than none. He argued that authority rests on an agreement among individuals who surrender their freedom to a sovereign for security, with no divine right in it. The book founded the social contract and the secular justification of the state.",
 now="Every trade of liberty for security, from emergency powers to surveillance, is argued in Hobbes's terms. A 'Hobbesian' world is the realist's description of international politics.",
 story="English philosopher Thomas Hobbes, in exile in Paris, published 'Leviathan' in 1651. Life without government, he wrote, would be 'nasty, brutish, and short'. People escape it by agreeing to obey a sovereign who keeps the peace.",
 why="The English Civil War and a century of religious war had convinced Hobbes that the breakdown of order was the worst political evil.",
 then="English philosopher John Locke and Genevan philosopher Jean-Jacques Rousseau answered with contracts that limited the sovereign or placed power in the people. The clergy attacked Hobbes's materialism for a century.",
 fact="Hobbes tutored the future Charles II in mathematics in Paris and returned to England the year the book appeared."),
"eu165336": dict(
 bluf="Oliver Cromwell dissolved a Parliament that could agree on no constitution and ruled as Lord Protector with the army behind him. He gave Protestant sects toleration, readmitted the Jews and conquered Ireland by massacre. The regime collapsed within two years of his death, and Britain kept a lasting suspicion of standing armies.",
 now="The Protectorate showed that a revolution against royal tyranny could end in rule by a general. Its memory brought the monarchy back on Parliament's terms and kept the army under Parliament's control.",
 story="Lord General Oliver Cromwell dissolved the Rump Parliament by force in April 1653 and took the title Lord Protector in December. He governed with a council and, for a time, through major-generals in the regions. Theatres closed and Christmas was suppressed.",
 why="Parliament had failed to agree on a constitution or on reforms. The army was the real power and Cromwell commanded it.",
 then="Jews were readmitted to England in 1656. The conquest of Ireland in 1649-53, with massacres at Drogheda and Wexford, transferred most Irish land to Protestants. The regime fell after Cromwell's death in 1658.",
 fact="Cromwell's body was dug up after the Restoration and hanged at Tyburn; his head stayed on a spike for twenty years."),
"d-pyrenees": dict(
 bluf="French chief minister Cardinal Mazarin wanted to close a war with Spain that had run on after Westphalia and to bind the Spanish succession to France. He got Roussillon and Artois and a Spanish princess for French King Louis XIV. France replaced Spain as Europe's leading power, and the marriage set up the succession war of 1701.",
 now="The Franco-Spanish border still follows the 1659 line, which cut Catalonia in two. The marriage clause produced the War of the Spanish Succession forty years later.",
 story="France and Spain had fought on after Westphalia until France, with English help, beat Spain at the Dunes in 1658. On Pheasant Island in the Bidasoa, French chief minister Cardinal Mazarin and Spanish chief minister Luis de Haro signed peace on 7 November 1659. Spain ceded Roussillon and Artois. Louis XIV was to marry Maria Theresa, daughter of Spanish King Philip IV.",
 why="Both treasuries were empty. France held the military advantage.",
 then="Maria Theresa renounced her Spanish rights in return for a dowry Spain never paid, which the Bourbons used as grounds to claim the throne in 1700.",
 fact="Pheasant Island is still administered by France and Spain in alternate six-month turns."),
"eu166037": dict(
 bluf="After Cromwell's death the republic fell apart, and General George Monck marched on London to arrange elections. The new Parliament invited Charles II back from exile on its own terms. The monarchy returned without absolute power, and the unresolved questions of Catholicism and prerogative produced the revolution of 1688.",
 now="The Restoration settled that England would be governed by king and Parliament together rather than by either alone; the crown returned on Parliament's terms, and lost power steadily after 1689 while keeping the throne.",
 story="Oliver Cromwell died in September 1658 and his son Richard resigned within nine months. General George Monck marched his army from Scotland to London in early 1660 and forced new elections. The Convention Parliament invited Charles II home. He entered London on 29 May 1660, his thirtieth birthday.",
 why="Without Cromwell the army and Parliament could not agree on anything. The monarchy was the only settlement most of the political nation would accept.",
 then="The regicides were hunted and the Church of England re-established with penalties on dissenters. Charles II's Catholic sympathies and his brother James's open Catholicism revived fears of absolutism.",
 fact="Samuel Pepys's diary, begun in January 1660, records the Restoration from the inside."),
"d-royalsociety": dict(
 bluf="Twelve experimenters in London wanted a standing body to test claims about nature by demonstration rather than by authority. Charles II chartered it in 1662. The Society made the experimental method an institution, and its journal created the system of scientific publication that science still runs on.",
 now="Scientific journals and peer review descend from the Philosophical Transactions. The Society's motto, 'take nobody's word for it', is still the stance of science toward authority.",
 story="On 28 November 1660, after a lecture by architect Christopher Wren at Gresham College, twelve men agreed to found a society for experimental learning. Chemist Robert Boyle and diarist John Evelyn were among them. King Charles II granted a royal charter in 1662. The Philosophical Transactions began in 1665.",
 why="Peace after the civil wars freed men of means for experiment. Francis Bacon's call to build knowledge from observation had a following among them.",
 then="The Society's licence appeared on Isaac Newton's 'Principia' in 1687. Every national academy of science copied the model.",
 fact="The Philosophical Transactions is the oldest scientific journal still published."),
"eu166638": dict(
 bluf="A bakery fire in Pudding Lane spread through wooden houses after a dry summer and burned for four days. It destroyed 13,000 houses, 87 churches and St Paul's, and left most of the City homeless. London was rebuilt in brick and stone under a building act, and fire insurance was invented to cover the next one.",
 now="The Rebuilding Act of 1667 was the first modern building code and the fire produced the first fire insurance companies. Both are now standard furniture of every city.",
 story="The fire began in baker Thomas Farriner's shop in Pudding Lane early on 2 September 1666 and burned until 5 September, driven by an east wind. It destroyed about 13,200 houses, 87 parish churches and St Paul's Cathedral. Most of the City's 80,000 people lost their homes. A handful of deaths were recorded.",
 why="Tightly packed timber houses, a dry summer and a strong wind. The Lord Mayor refused to pull down houses to make firebreaks on the first night.",
 then="Architect Christopher Wren rebuilt St Paul's and 51 churches. The Rebuilding Act of 1667 required brick or stone and wider streets. The Fire Office of 1680 sold the first fire insurance.",
 fact="A French watchmaker confessed to starting the fire and was hanged, though he had been at sea when it began."),
"eu168339": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Grand Vizier Kara Mustafa marched on Vienna to take the Habsburg capital and the prestige that went with it. A relief army under Polish King Jan Sobieski broke the siege after two months. Ottoman expansion in Europe ended there; within sixteen years the Habsburgs held Hungary, and the question of who would inherit the Ottoman Balkans entered European politics.",
 now="1683 turned the Ottoman Empire from an expanding to a retreating power in Europe. The Habsburg conquest of Hungary that followed made Austria a Danubian empire and opened the Eastern Question.",
 story="Ottoman Grand Vizier Kara Mustafa besieged Vienna in July 1683 with an army of perhaps 150,000, encouraged by a Hungarian revolt against the Habsburgs. Holy Roman Emperor Leopold I fled the city. On 12 September a relief army of Habsburg, German and Polish troops under Polish King Jan Sobieski broke the siege with a charge down the Kahlenberg.",
 why="Kara Mustafa wanted the capital and the glory. French King Louis XIV, who wanted the Habsburgs weak, stayed out.",
 then="A Holy League of Austria, Poland, Venice and Russia drove the Ottomans back. At Karlowitz in 1699 the Ottomans gave up Hungary. Kara Mustafa was strangled on the sultan's orders that December.",
 fact="The charge of Sobieski's winged hussars is often called the largest cavalry charge in history."),
"eu168740": dict(
 bluf="English mathematician Isaac Newton set out to derive the planets' orbits from one law of force, at the urging of astronomer Edmond Halley. He succeeded: three laws of motion and universal gravitation explained Kepler's orbits, the tides and the comets. The universe appeared predictable, and the Enlightenment took the book as its model of reason finding law in nature.",
 now="Newton gave the Enlightenment its model: if the heavens obey laws, so might society, economics and politics. His laws still launch rockets.",
 story="Astronomer Edmond Halley visited Cambridge in 1684 to ask what orbit an inverse-square force would produce; English mathematician Isaac Newton answered that he had worked it out, an ellipse. Halley paid for the printing. The 'Principia' of 1687 set out three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation.",
 why="Kepler and Galileo had described how planets and falling bodies move. Newton supplied the why in one law.",
 then="Newtonian physics ruled science for two centuries. Thinkers looked for similar laws of society, the foundation of Enlightenment thought.",
 fact="Halley paid for the printing because the Royal Society had spent its budget on a history of fishes."),
"eu168841": dict(
 bluf="Seven English politicians invited Dutch ruler William of Orange to invade and remove the Catholic James II, and William wanted England's resources for his war on Louis XIV. James's army melted away and he fled; Parliament gave the crown to William and Mary on its own terms. Parliamentary supremacy, constitutional monarchy and a state that could borrow cheaply followed, the base of British power for a century and a model for the American constitution.",
 now="Britain's constitutional monarchy and the American founders' idea of limited government rest on the settlement of 1689. The fiscal state it produced outfought richer France for a century.",
 story="English King James II was promoting Catholics, suspending laws by prerogative and building a standing army. In June 1688 his son's birth promised a Catholic dynasty. Seven leading politicians invited Dutch ruler William of Orange, husband of James's Protestant daughter Mary, to invade. William landed at Torbay in November with about 15,000 men. James fled to France.",
 why="James's Catholicism and his use of prerogative united Whigs and Tories against him. William needed England against Louis XIV.",
 trig="The birth of James's son in June 1688 and the acquittal of seven bishops who had refused to read his declaration of toleration.",
 then="The Bill of Rights of 1689 put taxes and the army under Parliament, which met every year from then on. The Bank of England of 1694 and a permanent national debt financed the wars against France. Ireland and Scotland were held by force.",
 fact="William's landing was the last successful invasion of England."),
"eu168942b": dict(
 bluf="English philosopher John Locke wrote to justify resistance to James II and the settlement that replaced him. He argued that government exists by consent to protect natural rights to life, liberty and property, and that a ruler who violates them may be resisted. The book became the theory of liberal government and of the American Revolution.",
 now="The Declaration of Independence restates Locke. Every argument over consent, property and the right to rebel is still conducted in his terms.",
 story="English philosopher John Locke published the 'Two Treatises of Government' anonymously in 1689, after returning from Dutch exile with William's fleet. People hold natural rights to life, liberty and property, he argued, and create governments by consent to protect them. A ruler who breaks the trust may be removed.",
 why="The revolution of 1688 needed a justification beyond Protestantism, and Locke had drafted one in exile.",
 then="Locke's argument became the standard defence of the 1688 settlement and, through the Enlightenment, of the American and French revolutions.",
 fact="Locke denied authorship of the book until his will."),
"eu168942": dict(
 bluf="Parliament wanted James II's successors bound by law before it handed them the crown. The Bill of Rights barred the monarch from suspending laws, taxing or keeping a peacetime army without Parliament's consent. Parliament became the centre of British government, and monarchs had to govern through ministers who could command it.",
 now="Because Parliament had to approve money and the army every year, the crown had to govern through ministers with a majority, which is how cabinet government and the prime minister arose.",
 story="The Convention Parliament presented the Declaration of Rights to William and Mary in February 1689 as the condition of their crown, and enacted it as the Bill of Rights in December. It barred the suspension of laws, taxation and a peacetime army without Parliament's consent and protected free elections and free speech in Parliament.",
 why="James II had ruled without Parliament and promoted Catholics. The new monarchs needed Parliament's money for war.",
 then="Parliament met every year from 1689. The Act of Settlement of 1701 fixed a Protestant succession.",
 fact="The American Bill of Rights copied its ban on cruel and unusual punishments almost word for word."),
"d-boyne": dict(
 bluf="The deposed James II used Catholic Ireland, with French support, as a base to win back his thrones; William III crossed to Ireland to finish him. William won at the Boyne and James fled to France. The Protestant succession was secure, and Ireland entered a century of Protestant Ascendancy that shut Catholics out of land and power.",
 now="The penal laws that followed the Boyne made religion the line of property and power in Ireland. The division runs through the Northern Ireland conflict and the Orange parades every 12 July.",
 story="James II landed in Ireland in 1689 with French troops. English King William III brought his own army over in 1690. On 1 July 1690, 11 July by the modern calendar, William's army crossed the Boyne and defeated James's Irish and French force. James left for France within days. The war ended at Limerick in 1691.",
 why="Ireland was the one kingdom where James still had an army and a Catholic majority.",
 then="The penal laws barred Catholics from Parliament, the professions and most land ownership for more than a century.",
 fact="The Pope backed William, because France was his enemy and William's ally was the Habsburg emperor."),
"eu169443": dict(
 bluf="William III needed money to fight Louis XIV, and London investors wanted a safe return backed by Parliament. The investors lent £1.2 million at 8 per cent and were chartered as the Bank of England. The state could now borrow cheaply against future taxes, and that fiscal strength let Britain outspend richer France for a century.",
 now="The Bank let Britain borrow at low rates against future taxes, which financed a century of war against France and is the model nearly every state's central bank follows.",
 story="English King William III needed money for the war against French King Louis XIV. In 1694 a group of investors led by Scottish merchant William Paterson lent the government £1.2 million at 8 per cent interest. In return they were chartered as the Bank of England, with the right to take deposits and issue notes.",
 why="Parliament's control of taxes after 1689 made government debt trustworthy for the first time.",
 then="A permanent national debt serviced by parliamentary taxes became the backbone of the British war state. The Bank became the lender of last resort to the banking system in the 1800s.",
 fact="The loan was fully subscribed in twelve days."),
"d-zenta": dict(
 bluf="Habsburg commander Prince Eugene of Savoy caught Sultan Mustafa II's army crossing the Tisza and destroyed it. Tens of thousands of Ottomans died against a few hundred Habsburgs. The Ottomans accepted the loss of Hungary at Karlowitz two years later, and Austria became the dominant power on the Danube.",
 now="Zenta ended Ottoman power in Hungary and made Austria a Danubian great power, which it remained until 1918; the lands it won are the Hungarian and Balkan provinces that later pulled it into the Balkan wars.",
 story="On 11 September 1697 Habsburg commander Prince Eugene of Savoy attacked the Ottoman army while it was crossing the Tisza at Zenta. Sultan Mustafa II watched from the far bank. The grand vizier and some 20,000 soldiers died; Habsburg losses were a few hundred.",
 why="The Habsburgs had been driving the Ottomans back since 1683, and Eugene moved faster than an army with its baggage half across a river.",
 then="The Ottomans made peace at Karlowitz in 1699.",
 fact="Eugene had been rejected by Louis XIV for French service as too small and sickly; he beat French armies for the next forty years."),
"d-karlowitz": dict(
 bluf="The Ottomans wanted an end to sixteen years of defeat; Austria, Poland, Venice and Russia wanted their conquests confirmed. At Karlowitz the Ottomans gave Hungary and Transylvania to Austria, Podolia to Poland and the Morea to Venice. The empire turned from expansion to defence, and Austria rose as the power on the Danube.",
 now="Karlowitz began the Ottoman retreat from Europe that ran to 1913. English and Dutch mediation set the precedent of European powers managing Ottoman affairs, the Eastern Question in its first form.",
 story="Mediated by England and the Dutch Republic, the treaty was signed on 26 January 1699 at Sremski Karlovci. Austria received Hungary and Transylvania, Poland Podolia and Venice the Morea and Dalmatia. Russia's separate truce gave it Azov in 1700.",
 why="Defeats from Vienna in 1683 to Zenta in 1697 had left the Ottomans without an army in the field.",
 then="The Ottomans fortified the Danube and recovered the Morea in 1718. Austria governed Hungary as a conquered province, which produced the revolts of 1703 and 1848.",
 fact="The negotiating pavilion had four doors so that no delegation entered after another."),
"eu170144": dict(
 bluf="French King Louis XIV accepted the whole Spanish empire for his grandson and refused to rule out the union of the two crowns; Britain, the Dutch and Austria went to war to prevent it. Twelve years of war kept the crowns apart and left Philip V in Madrid. Britain came out with Gibraltar, Minorca, the slave-trade contract and the position of leading naval and commercial power.",
 now="The balance of power became the stated principle of European politics at Utrecht, and Britain its manager for two centuries. Spain's Bourbon dynasty still reigns.",
 story="Spanish King Charles II died childless in November 1700 and left his empire to Philip of Anjou, grandson of French King Louis XIV. Louis accepted. Britain, the Dutch Republic and Austria backed a Habsburg claimant and declared war in 1702. The Duke of Marlborough's victories from Blenheim in 1704 broke France's reputation. Peace came at Utrecht in 1713 and Rastatt in 1714.",
 why="A single Bourbon family ruling France, Spain and the Americas would have dominated Europe and its trade.",
 trig="Charles II's will of 1700 and Louis XIV's acceptance of it.",
 then="Philip V kept Spain and renounced France. Britain took Gibraltar, Minorca and the asiento, the contract to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America. Catalonia, which had backed the Habsburg, lost its institutions in 1714.",
 fact="Catalonia's national day, 11 September, marks the fall of Barcelona in 1714."),
"d-stpetersburg": dict(
 bluf="Russian Tsar Peter I wanted a Baltic port and a capital free of Moscow's traditions. He founded a city on land just taken from Sweden and moved the court, the navy and the civil service there. Russia faced Europe by decree for two centuries, and the gap between a westernised elite and the peasant interior became the standing fact of Russian politics.",
 now="A capital on the Baltic turned Russia toward Europe by decree: the court, the navy and the civil service were built there, and the city's westernised elite governed an eastern empire for two centuries, a divide Russian politics still carries.",
 story="On 27 May 1703 Russian Tsar Peter I laid the foundations of the Peter and Paul Fortress on the Neva, on marshland taken from Sweden weeks before. Tens of thousands of conscripted labourers built the city; many died of disease. It became the capital in 1712.",
 why="Russia had no port on the Baltic. Peter wanted the navy, the trade and the distance from Moscow.",
 then="St Petersburg remained the capital until 1918. The nobility was ordered to build houses there and did.",
 fact="Peter banned stone building elsewhere in Russia for a time so that every mason would come to the new city."),
"eu170745": dict(
 bluf="Scotland was near bankruptcy after the Darien colony failed, and England wanted to stop Scotland choosing a different king and allying with France. The Acts of Union made one state, one parliament and one market. Scotland gained the empire's trade and England its northern border, and Great Britain became the base of imperial power.",
 now="The union gave Scots the empire as a career and Britain a secure island. Scotland voted to stay in 2014; the question remains open and Brexit sharpened it.",
 story="Scotland's Darien scheme, a colony on the isthmus of Panama, had failed by 1700 and taken a quarter of the country's liquid capital with it. The English Parliament's Act of Settlement of 1701 fixed a Hanoverian succession that Scotland had not agreed to. The Acts of Union of 1707 merged the two parliaments at Westminster.",
 why="Scotland needed England's trade and empire. England needed Scotland bound to the Protestant succession.",
 then="Scots served the empire out of proportion to their numbers as soldiers, administrators, doctors and merchants. The Scottish Enlightenment followed within a generation.",
 fact="England paid Scotland the Equivalent, nearly £400,000, partly to compensate Darien's investors."),
"eu170946": dict(
 bluf="Swedish King Charles XII invaded Russia to knock Tsar Peter I out of the Great Northern War. His army was drawn south into Ukraine, starved through a winter and destroyed at Poltava. Sweden ceased to be a great power, Russia took the Baltic coast and became the dominant state in eastern Europe, and the Cossack hetman who had joined Sweden lost Ukraine's autonomy.",
 now="Poltava ended Sweden as a great power and made Russia the dominant state in the Baltic and eastern Europe, the position it held until 1917 and sought again after 1945.",
 story="Swedish King Charles XII invaded Russia in 1708 and turned south into Ukraine, where Cossack Hetman Ivan Mazepa had joined him. The winter of 1708-09 killed thousands of his men. On 8 July 1709 Russian Tsar Peter I's larger army crushed the Swedes at Poltava. The survivors surrendered at Perevolochna three days later.",
 why="Sweden held the Baltic coast and Peter wanted it. Charles gambled on a march to Moscow and lost his supply train.",
 then="Russia won the Baltic coast at Nystad in 1721 and Peter took the title of emperor. Moscow cut back Cossack autonomy in Ukraine.",
 fact="In Ukraine the battle is remembered as a lost chance for independence, and Mazepa remains contested."),
"eu171347": dict(
 bluf="Britain's Tory government wanted out of a war it had already won at sea and in trade. The Utrecht treaties kept France and Spain under separate crowns and gave Britain Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland and the slave-trade contract. The balance of power was written into a treaty for the first time, and Britain became its manager.",
 now="Utrecht made the balance of power an explicit principle of diplomacy and Britain the leading maritime power. Gibraltar is still a British territory and a Spanish grievance.",
 story="The treaties signed at Utrecht in April 1713 ended the War of the Spanish Succession. Spanish King Philip V kept Spain and renounced the French succession. Britain gained Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland, Nova Scotia and the asiento. Austria took the Spanish Netherlands and Spanish Italy.",
 why="Twelve years of war had exhausted every power. Britain's new Tory ministry made peace without its allies.",
 then="Savoy and Prussia rose in rank as kingdoms. France was contained and stayed strong. The asiento made Britain the largest carrier in the Atlantic slave trade.",
 fact="The treaty's preamble names the balance of power as its purpose, the first treaty to do so."),
"eu172048": dict(
 bluf="The South Sea Company offered to take over Britain's national debt in exchange for shares and trading rights, and bribed ministers and MPs to get the deal. Its shares rose from £128 to £1,000 in eight months and collapsed by autumn. Parliament restricted new companies for a century, and Robert Walpole rose to power by cleaning up.",
 now="The Bubble Act of 1720 kept the joint-stock company rare in Britain until 1825. Walpole's management of the crash made him the first prime minister.",
 story="The South Sea Company proposed in January 1720 to convert much of the national debt into its own shares, with ministers and MPs holding stock they had paid nothing for. Shares rose from about £128 in January to about £1,000 in August and fell below £200 by December. Thousands were ruined.",
 why="Government debt converted into shares, insiders talking up the price and a public that had seen the Mississippi bubble in Paris make fortunes that spring.",
 then="Parliament passed the Bubble Act restricting new companies. Robert Walpole protected the court and the ministry and emerged as the dominant minister for twenty years.",
 fact="Isaac Newton lost a fortune and is reported to have said he could calculate the motions of the stars but not the madness of men."),
"eu172149": dict(
 bluf="Russian Tsar Peter I set out to make Russia a European military power by copying Western methods and forcing them on his nobles. He built a navy, reorganised the army and the state, put the Church under a government department and beat Sweden. Russia became a great power, and its model of coercive modernisation from the top, which later rulers repeated, began with him.",
 now="Peter made Russia a European great power by force from above and left a westernised elite ruling a peasant country. Russia still argues over whether it is European, Eurasian or its own civilisation, and Vladimir Putin has compared himself to Peter.",
 story="Russian Tsar Peter I toured Western Europe in 1697-98, partly incognito, working in Dutch and English shipyards. At home he ordered nobles to shave and dress in European style, built a navy and a new army, replaced the patriarch with a Holy Synod and founded St Petersburg. After the Treaty of Nystad with Sweden in 1721 the Senate proclaimed him Emperor of All Russia.",
 why="Russia had lost to Sweden at Narva in 1700 and Peter concluded that survival required Western arms, ships and administration.",
 trig="The Treaty of Nystad in September 1721 confirmed Russia's conquest of the Baltic coast.",
 then="The Table of Ranks of 1722 made state service the basis of noble rank. Serfdom deepened to pay for it. The elite westernised and the peasants did not, a divide that held until 1917.",
 fact="Peter taxed beards and issued tokens to prove the tax was paid."),
"eu174050": dict(
 bluf="Prussian King Frederick II saw a rich province to be had while Maria Theresa's right to inherit the Habsburg lands was in dispute, and took Silesia by surprise. He kept it through two wars. Prussia became a great power, Austria reformed itself to compete, and the two fought for Germany until 1866.",
 now="The Austro-Prussian rivalry over Germany ran from Silesia to Königgrätz in 1866. Frederick showed that a small state with a good army could seize territory and keep it, the lesson every later aggressor cited.",
 story="Holy Roman Emperor Charles VI died in October 1740 after years spent winning promises, the Pragmatic Sanction, that his daughter Maria Theresa could inherit. Prussian King Frederick II, on the throne five months, invaded Silesia in December with 27,000 men. France, Bavaria and Spain joined the attack. Maria Theresa rallied Hungary and survived; Silesia stayed Prussian.",
 why="Silesia was rich, populous and Protestant. Frederick had a trained army, a full treasury and a rival whose title was contested.",
 trig="The death of Charles VI on 20 October 1740.",
 then="The War of the Austrian Succession to 1748 and the Seven Years' War to 1763 confirmed Prussia's hold. Maria Theresa reformed Austria's army, taxes and administration in response.",
 fact="Silesia became Polish in 1945, and its German population was expelled."),
"eu175551": dict(
 bluf="An earthquake off the Portuguese coast, a tsunami and fires destroyed Lisbon on All Saints' Day 1755 and killed perhaps 30,000 to 50,000 people. Chief minister the Marquis of Pombal rebuilt the city on a plan and surveyed every parish about the shock. Disaster became a matter for the state and for science, and the Enlightenment lost its confidence in a benevolent order.",
 now="The earthquake destroyed a capital and the certainty that providence governed events: the state rebuilt Lisbon on a plan and the Enlightenment argued over whether a good God could permit it, which made disaster a matter for governments and science.",
 story="On the morning of 1 November 1755 an earthquake struck Lisbon while the churches were full for All Saints' Day. A tsunami and fires followed. The royal palace, the opera house and most of the lower city were destroyed. Portuguese chief minister the Marquis of Pombal took charge of relief and rebuilding.",
 why="A great earthquake on the sea floor off Portugal.",
 then="Pombal rebuilt central Lisbon on a grid with earthquake-resistant timber frames and sent a questionnaire to every parish, an early seismological survey. French writer Voltaire mocked the doctrine that all is for the best in 'Candide'.",
 fact="Pombal's reported order was 'bury the dead and feed the living'."),
"eu175652": dict(
 bluf="Britain and France fought for North America and India; Austria fought to recover Silesia from Prussia. Britain won Canada, India and command of the seas, and Prussia kept Silesia. The debts Britain ran up produced the taxes that lost it the American colonies, and France's defeat and debts pushed it toward revolution.",
 now="The war began a century and a half of British primacy and set up both the American and the French revolutions through the debts it left.",
 story="Britain and France were already fighting in the Ohio valley when Prussian King Frederick II invaded Saxony in August 1756. Austria, France and Russia fought Prussia in Germany; Britain fought France in North America, India, West Africa, the Caribbean and at sea. British forces took Quebec in 1759 and broke French power in India.",
 why="Colonial rivalry between Britain and France came first. Austria wanted Silesia back and had built an alliance with France and Russia to get it.",
 then="At Paris in 1763 France gave Canada and the land east of the Mississippi to Britain and Louisiana to Spain. Prussia kept Silesia. Britain's national debt nearly doubled, and Parliament turned to the colonies to pay.",
 fact="Fighting ran on four continents and three oceans, which is why it is often called the first world war."),
"d-halley": dict(
 bluf="Astronomer Edmond Halley used Newton's laws to argue that the comets of 1531, 1607 and 1682 were one object that would return in 1758. It returned on Christmas night, sixteen years after his death. A theory had forecast an event in the heavens decades ahead, and Newton's physics had no serious doubters left in Europe.",
 now="The comet's return made Newtonian science the accepted framework of educated Europe and prediction the test of a theory.",
 story="English astronomer Edmond Halley argued in 1705 that three recorded comets were one body on a 76-year orbit and would return in 1758. Halley died in 1742. On Christmas night 1758 Saxon farmer and amateur astronomer Johann Palitzsch spotted it near Dresden. It passed closest to the Sun in March 1759.",
 why="Newton's gravity made it possible to compute a comet's orbit and the planets' pull on it.",
 then="The comet took Halley's name. It returned in 1835, 1910 and 1986 and is due in 2061.",
 fact="Three French mathematicians had refined the prediction to within a month using Jupiter's and Saturn's pull."),
"eu176253b": dict(
 bluf="Genevan philosopher Jean-Jacques Rousseau set out to show what could make political power legitimate once kings and tradition were ruled out. His answer was the general will of the people, formed by a social contract. Popular sovereignty became the French Revolution's doctrine, and the general will, which may override the individual, has served democrats and dictators alike.",
 now="The French Revolution took its doctrine of popular sovereignty from the book. Every argument over majority rule against individual rights returns to the general will.",
 story="Genevan philosopher Jean-Jacques Rousseau published 'The Social Contract' in 1762. Legitimate government, he argued, rests on a contract in which citizens together form a sovereign general will. The book was banned in Paris and burned in Geneva.",
 why="Enlightenment writers were asking what made authority legitimate, and Rousseau rejected both divine right and Hobbes's surrender to a sovereign.",
 then="The Jacobins governed in the name of the general will in 1793. The book was carried in Robespierre's pocket, by his own account.",
 fact="Rousseau opened with the line that man is born free and is everywhere in chains."),
"eu176253": dict(
 bluf="Catherine, a German princess married to Russian Tsar Peter III, built alliances among the guards regiments and overthrew her husband six months into his reign. She ruled 34 years, took Crimea and the Black Sea coast and the largest share of partitioned Poland, and deepened serfdom to pay for it. Russia became a dominant European power under an Enlightenment vocabulary and an autocratic state.",
 now="Catherine's conquest of Crimea and southern Ukraine is the history Russian nationalists cite to claim them. Her reign fixed the pattern of an enlightened autocracy that reformed everything except its own power.",
 story="Russian Tsar Peter III had alienated the army and the Church within months of his accession. On 9 July 1762 guards regiments loyal to his wife, Catherine, proclaimed her empress; Peter abdicated and died in custody days later. Empress Catherine II corresponded with Voltaire and Diderot and pushed Russia's borders south and west.",
 why="Peter had ended a winning war against Prussia and mocked the Orthodox Church. Catherine had the guards and the court.",
 then="She annexed Crimea in 1783, founded Odessa, took the largest share of Poland in three partitions and crushed the Pugachev revolt of 1773-75. Serfdom spread into the conquered south.",
 fact="Catherine was born Sophie of Anhalt-Zerbst, a minor German princess, and took the Russian throne by overthrowing her husband."),
}
